# Line endings and binary safety for substrate2.
#
# Two problems this prevents, both of which have actually bitten this codebase:
#
# 1. CRLF drift. A Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites LF to CRLF, which
#    changes file bytes. The byte-parity harness LF-normalises before hashing so it is
#    immune, but nothing else is, and the open conformance corpus lost a reproducible-build
#    check to exactly this in July 2026. Pin LF in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# 2. Binary corruption of the RFC 3161 fixture. tests/fixtures/*.der is a REAL timestamp
#    token; if git ever applied end-of-line translation to it the DER would be corrupted and
#    the signature would stop verifying. Today it survives only because git's binary
#    heuristic happens to fire (`git check-attr` reports "unspecified"). That is a guess, not
#    a rule. Make it a rule.
*.der binary
*.b64 -text

# Conformance vectors: the manifest pins per-file sha256 over LF-normalised bytes, so these
# must never be translated either.
*.json text eol=lf
